<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banc d'Arguin National Park</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1976">1976</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Banc d'Arguin National Park is a protected area on the Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nouakchott">Nouakchott</a> and Nouadhibou. Created in 1976, it covers sand dunes, coastal swamps, islands and shallow waters that host millions of migratory birds, and its Imraguen fishermen still use traditional methods. It has been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1989.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/banc-darguin-national-park.jpg" alt="Banc d'Arguin National Park" width="320" height="460" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bancdarguin_map_lg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The park lies on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nouakchott">Nouakchott</a> and Nouadhibou, around the Bay of Arguin, which is the former mouth of the Tamanrasset River. Battering surf and shifting sand banks run along the shoreline, and the nesting islands include Tidra, Niroumi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nair">Nair</a>, Kijji and Arguim. Vegetation on the coastal dunes is rare, though tamarisk bushes and dwarf acacias grow at the foot of ridges. The climate is humid but temperate, shaped by trade winds from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a>, and rainfall is minimal.</p><p>Geologically the northern bank is a flat-topped platform of carbonate deposits built up in water less than 10 m deep, made largely of barnacle and mollusc remains. Offshore, mud wedges have grown continuously over the past 9,000 years, since the sea flooded the shelf early in the Holocene. The surrounding waters are among the richest fishing waters of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Because of its rich fishery and strategic position the territory was coveted by the European powers Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and Holland. The Portuguese established a trading fort at Arguin in 1455, the first Portuguese installation south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-bojador">Cape Bojador</a>. Having little success in finding gold, they quickly turned to dealing in slaves, and in the mid-15th century as many as 1,000 slaves a year were exported from Arguin to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-tome">São Tomé</a>. After the union of the Portuguese and Spanish crowns in 1580 the Spaniards became dominant, and in 1638 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> replaced them and began exploiting the gum arabic trade. By 1678 the French had driven out the Dutch.</p><p>On 2 July 1816 the French frigate La Méduse ran aground at high tide off the bank. The account of the survivors Alexandre Corréard and Jean-Baptiste-Henri Savigny inspired Théodore Géricault's painting The Raft of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medusa">Medusa</a>, completed in 1819 and now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The park was established in 1976 as a nature reserve to protect its natural resources and fisheries. Its mudflats host more than two million migrant waders from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>, and between 25,000 and 40,000 pairs of 15 nesting species form the largest colonies of water birds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. At least 108 bird species have been recorded, and marine mammals include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> monk seals and dolphins. About 500 Imraguen live in seven villages inside the park and fish by traditional methods, and no motor boats are allowed. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> and an Important Bird Area.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Banc d'Arguin National Park” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The fort was later abandoned, and the bay is now part of Mauritania's <b>Banc d'Arguin National Park</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/arguin">Arguin</a></li></ul></section>
